import{_ as t,a,o as r,M as n}from"./chunks/framework.DYNaguLt.js";const o={},u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Zen","description":"A contemplation on the impermanence of mountains.","frontmatter":{"title":"Zen","subtitle":"and the art of software maintenance","illustration":"enso","list":{"title":"Zen and the art of software maintenance"},"description":"A contemplation on the impermanence of mountains.","topics":["flow"],"date":"2026-02-14T00:00:00.000Z","head":[["meta",{"name":"description","content":"A contemplation on the impermanence of mountains."}],["meta",{"name":"keywords","content":"software zen"}]],"wordCount":1534,"readingTime":"8 minute read"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"on/zen-and-the-art-of-software-maintenance/index.md","filePath":"on/zen-and-the-art-of-software-maintenance/index.md"}'),i={name:"on/zen-and-the-art-of-software-maintenance/index.md"};function s(l,e,m,f,p,c){return r(),a("div",null,e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<aside><p><strong>Author&#39;s note</strong>: What follows should in no way be associated with that great body of information relating to metaphysical philosophy. It&#39;s not very informative about software, either.</p></aside><p>From the outside, a department of software engineering may seem an unlikely place in which to find a spiritual experience. Deskbound in stark separation from nature and ceaselessly directed to perform exercises in logic of ever-increasing difficulty, the apocryphal developer sits or stands in a state of perpetual turmoil, as mind battles for supremacy over bug. Indeed, an office is not a place in which it is possible to practice <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zazen" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>zazen</em> <code>坐禅</code></a> for any length of time, insofar as practical considerations (like not being fired) must be taken into account.</p><p>Writing in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Kapleau#Bibliography" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Three Pillars of Zen</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Kapleau" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Philip Kapleau</a> described the three aims of <em>zazen</em> as:</p><ul><li><em>joriki</em> <code>定力</code>, the development of the power of concentration;</li><li><em>kenshō</em> <code>見性</code>, the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Satori" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>satori</em></a> awakening;</li><li>and <em>mujōdō-no-taigen</em> <code>無上道体現</code>, the actualisation of the way in daily life.</li></ul><p>The first of these, <em>joriki</em>, <em>the power of concentration</em>, which Kapleau described as &quot;the state in which the mind becomes like clear, still water&quot;, is the focus of much attention within the annals and treatises of software engineering, although there it is known by a different name: <em>flow</em>.</p><p>From the seminal work of psychologist <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mihaly_Csikszentmihalyi" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi</a>, <em>flow</em> is an optimal state of intrinsic motivation, characterised by a feeling of absorption where the person is fully immersed in what they are doing.</p><blockquote><p>... when an activity is thoroughly engrossing, there is not enough attention left over to allow a person to consider either the past or the future, or any other temporarily irrelevant stimuli. One item that disappears from awareness deserves special mention, because in normal life we spend so much time thinking about it: our own self. <a href="https://files.blogs.baruch.cuny.edu/wp-content/blogs.dir/2418/files/2013/04/Mihaly-Csikszentmihalyi-Flow.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">flow: The Psychology of Optimal Experience</a></p></blockquote><p>With <a href="https://ics.uci.edu/~gmark/chi08-mark.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">demonstrably positive outcomes</a> for the happiness of the engineer and the productivity of the business, the attainment of a <em>flow state</em>, that being the dissolution of the ego and freedom from the the temporally mundane, is centrally important to both disciplines.</p><blockquote><p>When you sit earnestly and egolessly in accordance with the instructions of a competent teacher — with your mind fully conscious yet as free of thought as a pure white sheet of paper is unmarred by a blemish — there is an unfoldment of your intrinsically pure Buddha-nature whether you have had satori or not. <a href="https://vinaire.me/wp-content/uploads/2023/03/kapleaupillars.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Three Pillars of Zen</a></p></blockquote><p>The importance of freedom of the mind from the prosaic notwithstanding, the ultimate realisation of awakening is found within the minutiae of daily existence. Elaborating on <em>mujōdō-no-taigen</em>, in a reflection on the <a href="https://www.sacred-texts.com/bud/zen/mumonkan.htm" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Mumonkan</em></a>, Kapleau says:</p><blockquote><p>Let us now consider Mumon&#39;s comment. He begins by saying: &quot;In the practice of Zen ... &quot; Zazen, receiving dokusan ... hearing teisho - these are all Zen practice. Being attentive in the details of your daily life is also training in Zen. When your life and Zen are one you are truly living Zen. Unless it accords with your everyday activities Zen is merely an embellishment. You must be careful not to flaunt Zen but to blend it unpretentiously into your life. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_Kapleau#Bibliography" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Three Pillars of Zen</em></a></p></blockquote><p>In the <a href="https://ashidakim.com/zenkoans/zenindex.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>101 Zen Stories</em></a> translation of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shasekish%C5%AB" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Shasekishū</em> <code>沙石集</code></a> collection of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Koan" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>kōans</em> <code>公案</code></a>, the moment of sudden awakening is often to be found within the distinctly mundane. The <a href="https://ashidakim.com/zenkoans/96adropofwater.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">occasion of a bath</a>, a <a href="https://ashidakim.com/zenkoans/31everythingisbest.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">visit to the market</a>, a <a href="https://ashidakim.com/zenkoans/1acupoftea.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">cup of tea</a> — all of these moments can precipitate <em>kenshō</em>.</p><p>The <a href="https://ashidakim.com/zenkoans/35everyminutezen.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Every-Minute Zen</em></a> parable contains an expression of the importance of the relationship between the practice of Zen and the administration of daily life.</p><blockquote><p>Nan-in was visited by Tenno, who, having passed his apprenticeship, had become a teacher. The day happened to be rainy, so Tenno wore wooden clogs and carried an umbrella. After greeting him Nan-in remarked: &quot;I suppose you left your wooden clogs in the vestibule. I want to know if your umbrella is on the right or left side of the clogs.&quot; <br> Tenno, confused, had no instant answer. He realized that he was unable to carry his Zen every minute. He became Nan-in&#39;s pupil, and he studied six more years to accomplish his every-minute Zen. <a href="https://ashidakim.com/zenkoans/35everyminutezen.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">101 Zen Stories</a></p></blockquote><p>The reach of <em>mujōdō-no-taigen</em> is as expansive as the entire realm of human existence. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ikebana" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Ikebana</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ink_wash_painting" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>sumi-e</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haiku" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>haiku</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_calligraphy" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>shodō</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_dry_garden" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>karesansui</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_tea_ceremony" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>chadō</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ky%C5%ABd%C5%8D" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>kyūdō</em></a>, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bushido" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>bushidō</em></a>; from flower arrangement, painting, poetry, calligraphy, gardening and cups of tea, to the practice of archery and the spiritual experience of the warrior.</p><p>Described by <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>The New York Times</em></a> as &quot;the profession&#39;s defining treatise&quot;, <a href="https://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/taocp.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>The Art of Computer Programming</em></a> is prefaced with an appeal to an <em>artistic</em> consideration of software in addition to the more obviously intellectual.</p><blockquote><p>The process of preparing programs for a digital computer is especially attractive, not only because it can be economically and scientifically rewarding, but also because it can be an aesthetic experience much like composing poetry or music. <a href="https://www-cs-faculty.stanford.edu/~knuth/taocp.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">The Art of Computer Programming</a></p></blockquote><p>Legendary seventeenth century <em>haiku</em> master <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matsuo_Bash%C5%8D" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Matsuo Bashō</a>, himself a student and practitioner of Zen, considered taking monastic vows yet ultimately could not renounce his attachment to poetry, which is to this day an enduring source of inspiration to many Zen adherents.</p><blockquote><p>inside the temple <br> visitors cannot know <br> cherries are blooming <a href="https://thehaikufoundation.org/omeka/items/show/206" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Bashō: The Complete Haiku</a></p></blockquote><p>The very contemplation of software may in itself offer an opportunity to meditate on <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Impermanence" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>mujō</em> <code>無常</code></a>, <em>impermanence</em>, the transience of life and nature, that being one of the three fundamental characteristics of existence <sup class="footnote-ref"><a href="#fn1" id="fnref1">[1]</a></sup> expressed in Buddhism. Software is a landscape littered with expressions which capture the inherent likenesses to phenomena of the natural order, of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Systems_development_life_cycle" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>lifecycles</em></a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_ecosystem" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>ecosystems</em></a>, of <a href="https://web.mit.edu/6.005/www/fa15/classes/09-immutability/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>mutation</em></a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Data_degradation" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>decay</em></a>. It is in the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emergence" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>emergent</em></a> property of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nonlinear_system" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>non-linearity</em></a> that simple micro-interactions can create complex macro-behaviours, and sufficiently dynamic systems can seem uncannily <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Conway&#39;s_Game_of_Life" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>organic</em></a>.</p><p>In <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sh%C5%8Db%C5%8Dgenz%C5%8D" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>Shōbōgenzō</em></a>, the founder of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C5%8Dt%C5%8D" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Sōtō</a> school <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/D%C5%8Dgen" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Dōgen Zenji</a> considers impermanence (even of the <em>inorganic</em>) as <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buddha-nature" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>busshō</em> <code>佛性</code></a> or &quot;Buddha-nature&quot;.</p><blockquote><p>... the very impermanence of grass and tree, thicket and forest is the Buddha-nature. The very impermanence of people and things, body and mind is the Buddha-nature. Lands and nations, mountains and rivers are impermanent because they are Buddha-nature. <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sh%C5%8Db%C5%8Dgenz%C5%8D#References" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Shōbōgenzō</a></p></blockquote><p>Musing proverbially on the nature of impermanence, <a href="https://ilya.grigorik.com/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Ilya Grigorik</a> notes of the &quot;interim&quot; solution proposed to the looming issue of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/IPv4_address_exhaustion" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">IPv4 address depletion</a> in <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1631" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RFC 1631</a>.</p><blockquote><p>... as it often happens, there is nothing more permanent than a temporary solution. Not only did the NAT devices resolve the immediate problem, but they also quickly became a ubiquitous component of many corporate and home proxies and routers, security appliances, firewalls, and dozens of other hardware and software devices. NAT middleboxes are no longer a temporary solution; rather, they have become an ossified part of the Internet infrastructure. <a href="https://hpbn.co/building-blocks-of-udp/#udp-and-network-address-translators" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">High Performance Browser Networking</a></p></blockquote><p>The adage <em>nothing is more permanent than a temporary solution</em> has acquired the status of a <em>kōan</em> within the software engineering community. A cursory browse through <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/ProgrammerHumor/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>r/ProgrammerHumor</code></a> abounds with thoughtful reflections on the concept.</p><blockquote><p>I once worked with an internally developed tool called &quot;TODO: name of program&quot;. <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/ProgrammerHumor/comments/lyehvf/todo_fix_this_later/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">TODO Fix this later </a></p></blockquote><p>Deferment of this sort is referred to, metaphorically, as <em>technical debt</em>, and which, accordingly with its namesake, is often likened to a <em>mountain</em>.</p><blockquote><p>Every morning I take my pickaxe and start chiseling a little off. The next morning, the mountain hasn&#39;t moved; despite the grueling work, the difference is hardly noticeable. I long for the green field in the valley below. <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/ExperiencedDevs/comments/q7hmm9/breaking_the_mountain_technical_debt/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Breaking the Mountain: Technical Debt</a></p></blockquote><p>In <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Japanese_tea_ceremony" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>chanoyu</em></a> the concept of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ichi-go_ichi-e" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>ichi go ichi e</em> <code>一期一会</code></a> (<em>one time, one meeting</em>) encourages practitioners to be fully present during interactions. From the expression <a href="https://www.omotesenke.jp/english/chanoyu/glossary.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>ichigo ni ichido</em> <code>一期に一度</code></a> (<em>one chance in a lifetime</em>) of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edo_period" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Edo period</a> master <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sen_no_Riky%C5%AB" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Sen no Rikyū</a>, it emphasises the impermanence of life, encapsulating the Zen concept of cherishing each moment as unique and irreplaceable, that encounters will never occur in the same way again. The phrase is also used as an admonishment with students of <em>budō</em> who fail to carry through with a technique in the presence of some mistake or other. In this context, the understanding is that in a struggle between life and death, outside the confines of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dojo" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><em>dōjō</em></a>, there can be no chance to try again.</p><blockquote><p>There is surely nothing other than the single purpose of the present moment. A man’s whole life is a succession of moment after moment. If one fully understands the present moment, there will be nothing else to do, and nothing else to pursue. Live being true to the single purpose of the moment. <a href="https://www.themathesontrust.org/papers/fareasternreligions/mt-hagakure.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Hagakure</a></p></blockquote><p>Software, once written, resists change; the temporary workaround becomes the <em>de facto</em> core. Software <em>maintenance</em>, that is, the <em>Art of Changing Software</em>, offers the devotee boundless opportunity to seize moments in which to dissolve the self, to achieve <em>now</em> that which had been abandoned for <em>later</em>, and in so doing to contemplate the impermanence of mountains.</p><br><h3 id="footnotes" tabindex="-1">Footnotes <a class="header-anchor" href="#footnotes" aria-label="Permalink to “Footnotes”">​</a></h3><hr class="footnotes-sep"><section class="footnotes"><ol class="footnotes-list"><li id="fn1" class="footnote-item"><p>Along with <em>dhukka</em> (suffering) and <em>anatta</em> (non-self); <em>suffering</em> being the default state of the developer, as previously noted. <a href="#fnref1" class="footnote-backref">↩︎</a></p></li></ol></section>',35)]))}const h={$style:o},g=t(i,[["render",s],["__cssModules",h]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
